<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ignatius of Loyola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1491">1491</a>–<a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ignatius of Loyola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ignatius of Loyola (1491–1556) was a Basque nobleman and soldier who founded the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Badly wounded defending the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> against a Franco-Navarrese army in 1521, he underwent a religious conversion during his recovery and wrote the Spiritual Exercises. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> order, approved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> in 1540, became a leading force of the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, education and missions. He was canonised in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola.jpg" alt="Ignatius of Loyola" width="249" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ignatius_Loyola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was the chief power of the Catholic Counter-Reformation: <b>Ignatius of Loyola</b> founded the Society of Jesus, Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross reformed the Carmelites, and Spanish theologians shaped the Council of Trent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1512 a Castilian army under the Duke of Alba took Pamplona, and in 1521 a Franco-Navarrese army briefly retook the city, where the young <b>Ignatius of Loyola</b> was wounded defending the citadel, before its defeat at Noáin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jesuits, the most effective of the new orders, followed <b>Ignatius of Loyola</b>, whose Spiritual Exercises shaped their devotion and who vowed to serve only God and the Roman pontiff; they worked in rural parishes and as missionaries in the Americas and Asia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The reformers’ Catholic opponents, among them Johann Eck, Thomas More and <b>Ignatius of Loyola</b>, founder of the Jesuits, were as committed as they were.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ignatius of Loyola</b>, the Basque soldier turned founder of the Society of Jesus, died in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-31">31 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ignatius of Loyola</b>, who with six companions had founded the Society of Jesus and was its first Superior General, was canonized together with Francis Xavier, the Jesuit co-founder who led the first Christian mission to Japan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oratory of the Holy House of Loyola. Died in Camere di San Ignazio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.9,12.48&amp;year=1533" aria-label="Camere di San Ignazio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ignatius of Loyola"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.9,12.48&amp;year=1533">What was here in 1533? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Man was created to praise, reverence and serve God our Lord, and by this means to save his soul.&#8221;</p><footer>Spiritual Exercises (1548), the Principle and Foundation &#183; tr. Charles Coppens, 1916 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/70790/pg70790-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Accept, O Lord, all my liberty, accept my memory, my understanding and my will. Thou hast given me all these powers; I restore them all to Thee, and I deliver them entirely into Thy hands that Thou mayest direct them.&#8221;</p><footer>Spiritual Exercises (1548), Contemplation to Obtain Divine Love &#183; tr. Charles Coppens, 1916 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/70790/pg70790-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
